    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Highway Catfish</li><li>Described by Zheng, 1979</li><li>Size: 76 mm (3 in) SL</li><li>Temperature: 16.0–23.0°C</li><li>pH: 6.4–7.6</li><li>IUCN Red List: Not Evaluated</li><li>Etymology: the specific epithet comes from the Latin tres, meaning three, and linea, meaning line, in reference to the colour pattern.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<p>Dongjiang drainage (part of the Zhujiang [Pearl River] drainage) in southern China. Type locality: mountain stream in Luofu Shan, Boluo Xian, Guangdong Province, China.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<p>Found in hillstreams with moderate to swift current and a predominantly rocky bottom.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 16.0–23.0°C (60.8–73.4°F)</li><li>pH: 6.4–7.6</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<p>Males have an elongate genital papilla in front of the anal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<p>Unreported. There is but a single breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1028",
      "html": "<p>Tachysurus can be identified by an extrascapular without a laterosensory canal, and a pterotic with a broad lateral laminar expansion. It is among the most speciose catfish groups in East Asia and its members inhabit habitats ranging from fast-flowing hillstreams to large rivers.</p><p>Easily distinguished by its elongate body, long adipose fin and attractive colouration. Xanthic and albino forms exist.</p>"
    },
